    "created_at": "2018-02-21T14:37:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-02-21T14:37:23Z",
    "body": "This was implemented/removed part of #3045, but I disagree with the new wording: there is a difference between acknowledging that web scripting is currently synonymous with Javascript and explicitly *restricting* UAs to Javascript.  As it is now, the wording is particularly egregious -- it says on the one hand that if another language gains widespread implementation in browsers, the spec will be amended to support it, but on the other that any UAs implementing an alternate language before that point are necessarily non-compliant.  Not just striking out on their own without official support, but *non-compliant*.\n\nI get that the standard is meant to prevent a return to the bad old days, but this isn't features being implemented in incompatible ways, this is a standardized option that wil only be implemented in a minority of UAs.  Any authors writing for Dart or Elm or even WebAssembly know that, and will be very sure to have a working fallback (something Javascript-based sites often forget).\n\nUnless there is a compelling reason to restrict UAs to using Javascript, then that passage should be revisited.  If there is, then that reason should be made apparent.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
